incumbency", "Huizenga holds a clear fundraising edge, but outside groups have spent over $1.1M attacking him while McCann has drawn little outside help", "The only recent poll (D-sponsored GBAO, mid-September) shows McCann ahead by a point", "Expert ratings split between Tossup and slight R lean, and markets price Democratic odds near 48%"], "rationale": "A strong national environment for Democrats offsets the seat's Republican lean and the incumbent's advantages, while the lone recent poll shows McCann narrowly ahead despite Huizenga's cash edge. Ratings, markets, and polling all cluster around even, leaving a genuine tossup with a slight Democratic tilt.", "watch": "New polling beyond the single September survey, movement in outside spending, or a shift in the national midterm environment could break this race either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Bill Huizenga (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Michigan's 4th District in southwest Michigan carries a modest Republican lean, and incumbent Bill Huizenga is seeking another term against state Senator Sean McCann. The measurable evidence points to a coin-flip race. Ten expert ratings split between Tossup and a slight Republican advantage, prediction markets put Democratic chances near 48 percent, and the only recent poll, taken for Democratic allies in mid-September, showed McCann ahead by a single point.\n\nThe fundamentals give McCann a modest edge: a favorable national midterm environment appears to outweigh the district's lean and Huizenga's incumbency. Huizenga holds a clear money advantage, with $2.3 million on hand to McCann's $1.7 million, but outside groups have spent heavily against him while McCann has drawn little outside support, and a stock-trading contrast story has added friction for the incumbent. A Green Party candidate is also on the ballot. The forecast lands on a margin near zero, well within normal polling error.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5309, "margin": 0